well we turn now to another interesting  happy story an important conversation  about the challenges that women face in  the workplace deb lue knows firsthand  she rose through the ranks of the  male-dominated tech industry to become a  vice president at facebook where she  created the site's marketplace future  lou is now ceo of ancestry the site that  helps people track their family history  her new book is take back your power 10  new rules for women at work in it she  draws upon her own experience to help  women thrive in their careers deb liu  welcome thank you so much for inviting  me yes absolutely so in your book you  talk about taking back your power but  you start off talking about how you were  giving it away and how often times  that's the case explain well you know so  many times when you're in the workplace  you're giving away your power in these  small ways like an example is when you  go into a room and you don't say  anything you go to a meeting my friend  carol izzazaki calls us the  unintentional ridiculous strategy you  don't go into a room and think at this  meeting i'm going to add no value or in  this meeting i'm not going to say  anything i'm going to sit in the back  and just listen but you do that how many  times you leave a room and you've done  that and so you know one of the things  about taking back your power is really  kind of saying what are let's be  intentional what do i want to do here  and other people are taking about taking  your power also interrupting you and  when you don't say anything you're  letting them do that so it starts with  the little things acknowledging that  that maybe we do that sometimes  and then making a decision to speak up  yes it is a choice each time you're in a  room each time you're in a conversation  you're making choices and those choices  really matter to how people perceive you  especially in a workplace where you're  not the majority so outside of speaking  up is there one particular thing you  think women should be doing to take back  their power i think the biggest thing is  learning to ask you know tell the story  of several women who are super powerful  but they were passed over for positions  because they didn't ask they didn't say  hey can i be your successor you know one  woman maybe tom she she actually trained  four of her bosses reporting to willie  brown before she actually said hey maybe  i could take the job and billy brown  said to her i thought you'd never ask  and the thing was she didn't raise her  hand no  now you write that what happens at home  impacts your success  as much sometimes more than what happens  at work um you are a mother so you know  what it's like to balance both what is  the key to  thriving  at home and at work i mean the biggest  thing is you know cheryl has the quote  the most important career decision you  make is who you choose to marry and that  is absolutely true because your partner  matters so much to the day-to-day of  your life you know whether you're the  household manager whether you're taking  care of the kids in their summer camps  you know a full partnership is the most  important thing you know can you  negotiate a full partnership can you  actually have somebody who's standing  side by side with you building your  careers together or are you kind of  taking on the full load and actually  having to take on all the household work  what about for single parents i think  for single parents it's hard to  negotiate because now everything is on  you so one thing is looking for a  workplace looking for a manager that's  supportive looking for flexibility  because that's going to make the big  difference for you  you talk about i mean let's  think about young children and the  messages that they're exposed to when  they're very very young we've all seen  those onesies right the onesies will say  pretty like mommy smart like daddy so  the messaging that we see in films uh  with male action stars video games  usually males at the center of those  games um how does this impact women in  their careers much later on well you  know there's studies that show that when  you ask little boys and girls you know  who's someone smart they draw someone  just like them and then by the time  they're in elementary school they're six  seven eight as they get older they  actually start skewing towards males  because they're you know they're  acculturated to think okay someone who's  smarter than me someone who's a leader  what does a leader look like and i think  that that goes on in your career what  does what does if i ask you what does a  ceo look like in america i think you  would imagine one thing and it wouldn't  be someone like me and that's you know  something which i grew up with it never  occurred to me that i would ever reach  this place because that's not what i was  taught and i think those lessons kind of  build on themselves slowly and quietly  over many many years can i ask you a  question about ancestry.com uh because  it's a fascinating company i'm usually  interested in genealogy and ancestry and  i'm curious what kind of efforts you're  making in gathering data to help people  who are not of european descent so  people from the caribbean people from  asia latin america uh the middle east  what kind of databases you're putting  into ancestry to help them figure out  who they are well since i've been ceo  which is about 18 months we've been  really focusing on an effort called  ancestry for all and it's really how do  we make ancestry more accessible we have  incredible records from western european  countries but you know one thing we've  lacked is is more records from other  countries as well and so that's one of  our big initiatives over the next  several years is to gather records and  make it more accessible to everyone on  our platform  deborah liu thank you so much for  joining us thank you for the invitation  take back your power goes on sale next  tuesday
 

